        {
          "@type": "FAQPage",
          "mainEntity": [
            {
              "@type": "Question",
              "name": "Do we need our own ASNT Level III on staff to get UR Z17 approval?",
              "acceptedAnswer": {
                "@type": "Answer",
                "text": "Z17 requires a responsible supervisor qualified to a recognised NDT standard with two years of experience in the activity; it does not name a Level III. But where the firm runs an employer-based scheme under ASNT SNT-TC-1A, the operator certificates in the document pack only exist because a qualified Level III wrote the written practice and administered and graded the examinations. Firms without one on staff contract that role rather than skip it."
              }
            },
            {
              "@type": "Question",
              "name": "Can we start class thickness work while the approval is in process?",
              "acceptedAnswer": {
                "@type": "Answer",
                "text": "No. Where results are used by a surveyor in making decisions affecting classification, the provider must be approved and verified by that Society. Z17 also requires the report to include a copy of the Certificate of Approval, so there is no version of the deliverable that works before issue. Work done in the gap is not accepted and does not count as demonstration evidence."
              }
            },
            {
              "@type": "Question",
              "name": "Does each of our branch offices need its own approval?",
              "acceptedAnswer": {
                "@type": "Answer",
                "text": "Yes, with one exception. Where several servicing stations are owned by a given company, each station is assessed and approved, except where a manufacturer's ISO 9000-certified quality system with effective control of nominated agents and subsidiaries covers them under the specific clause. A second office does not inherit the first office's certificate by sharing a company name."
              }
            },
            {
              "@type": "Question",
              "name": "What happens if class finds our thickness gauge out of calibration?",
              "acceptedAnswer": {
                "@type": "Answer",
                "text": "Z17 requires the Society to assess and record the validity of previous measuring results when equipment is found not to conform, and to take appropriate action on the equipment affected. That reaches into reports already issued and accepted. Per-serial-number calibration and verification records, with the reference block identified, are what limits the damage to the instrument rather than the file."
              }
            },
            {
              "@type": "Question",
              "name": "How long does the whole approval sequence take?",
              "acceptedAnswer": {
                "@type": "Answer",
                "text": "The order is fixed — document review, office audit, witnessed demonstration, certificate — and the demonstration is the constraint, because it needs a live job on that society's classed tonnage with its surveyor present. Firms that assemble the document pack, personnel records and training evidence before submitting clear the review in one round instead of three, which is usually where the calendar is won or lost."
              }
            }
          ]
        }
